'''victimate†''' (vik′tim-āt), ''v. t.'' [< LL. ''victimatus'', pp. of ''victimare'' (> F. ''victimer''), sacrifice as a victim, < L. ''victima'', a victim: see ''victim''.] To sacrifice; immolate; victimize. ''Bullokar.''

'''victimization''' (vik″tim-i-zā′shọn), ''n.'' [< ''victimize'' + ''-ation''.] The act of victimizing, or the state of being victimized. Also spelled ''victimisation''.
{{smaller block|The general ''victimization'' of good people by bad, which is the leading “motif” of the story.
{{right|''Contemporary Rev.'', L. 365.}}}}

'''victimize''' (vik′tim-īz), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''victimized'', ppr. ''victimizing''. [< ''victim'' + ''-ize''.] To make a victim of; especially, to make the victim of a swindling transaction; dupe; cheat. Also spelled ''victimise''. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|Mrs. Boldero’s noble nephew, the present Strongitharm, . . . was ''victimized'' by his own uncle, and a most painful affair occurred between them at a game at “blind hookey.”
{{right|''Thackeray'', Philip, xxi.}}
A fascinating married man, ''victimized'' by a crazy wife, and ready to throw himself on the sympathies of womanhood in this affliction. {{right|''H. B. Stowe'', Oldtown, p. 512.}}
By submitting in turn to be ''victimized'', a party of children can secure, at a moderate cost to each, the zest of the malevolent feeling; and this I take to be the quintessence of play. {{right|''A. Bain'', Pop. Sci. Mo., XIII. 311.}}}}

'''victimizer''' (vik′tim-ī-zėr), ''n.'' [< ''victimize'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who victimizes; a swindler. Also spelled ''victimiser''.
{{smaller block|The invalid had a great hatred and secret terror of her ''victimizer''. {{right|''Thackeray'', Vanity Fair, xix.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
any kind; one who vanquishes another in any struggle, especially in war; one who defeats an enemy in battle; a conqueror.
{{smaller block|<poem>Pericles was a famous man of warre,
And ''victor'' eke, in nine great foughten fields.</poem>
{{right|''Gascoigne'', Steele Glas (ed. Arber), p. 64.}}
<poem>If your father had been ''victor'' there.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., iv. 1. 134.}}
<poem>In love, the ''victors'' from the vanquish’d fly;
They fly that wound, and they pursue that die.</poem>
{{right|''Waller'', To a Friend, on the Different Success of [their Loves.}}}}
'''2.''' One who ruins or destroys; a destroyer. [Rare or poetical.]
{{smaller block|<poem>There, ''victor'' of his health, of fortune, friends,
And fame, this lord of useless thousands ends.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Moral Essays, iii. 313.}}}}
{{smaller block|='''Syn. 1.''' ''Victor, Conqueror.'' A ''victor'' differs from a ''conqueror'' inasmuch as the latter achieves a complete success and conquers his opponent perhaps after a series of victories, while the ''victor'' is so called because of his success in a single or a particular contest, which may be otherwise barren of result to him. ''Victor'' is also applied to one who gains the day in a personal contest or competition, as in a race.}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Victorious.
{{smaller block|<poem>Despite thy ''victor'' sword and fire-new fortune,
Thy valour and thy heart, thou art a traitor.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, v. 3. 132.}}
<poem>Where ’s now their ''victor'' vaward wing,
Where Huntly, and where Home?</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', Marmion, vi. 33.}}}}

'''victor''' (vik′tọr), ''v. i.'' [< ''victor'', ''n.''] To play the victor; exult.
{{smaller block|<poem>To runne through all the pamphlets and the toyes
Which I haue seene in hands of ''Victoring'' Boyes.</poem>
{{right|''A. Holland'' (Davies, Scourge of Folly, p. 80). (''Davies.'')}}}}

