'''Victoria lawn.''' A kind of muslin used for fittings, and sometimes for women’s dresses.

'''Victorian''' (vik-tō′ri-an), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ''Victoria'' (see def.) + ''-an.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Of or pertaining to the reign of Victoria, Queen of Great Britain and Ireland, which began in 1837: as, the ''Victorian'' literature; the ''Victorian'' crown (see first cut under ''crown'').
{{smaller block|We can’t do anything better than go back to Queen Anne for our furniture. But in respect to women it’s quite different. We’ve got a ''Victorian'' type in that.
{{right|''Mrs. Oliphant'', The Ladies Lindores, II. xii.}}

In things specifically poetic he [Matthew Arnold] touched his readers less than any other ''Victorian'' poet of the first rank. {{right|''Athenæum'', April 21, 1888, p. 501.}}

The ''Victorian'' age has produced a plentiful crop of parodists in prose and in verse. {{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XVIII. 319.}}

Macaulay, the historian of the first ''Victorian'' period.
{{right|''Fortnightly Rev.'', N. S., XLI. 842.}}}}
'''2.''' Pertaining to Victoria in Australia.—'''Victorian bird-cherry.''' {{smaller|See ''Pimelea.''}}—'''Victorian bottle-tree.''' {{smaller|See ''Sterculia.''}}—'''Victorian bower-spinach.''' {{smaller|See ''Australian spinach'' (under ''spinach'').}}—'''Victorian cabbage-tree.''' {{smaller|See ''Livistona.''}}—'''Victorian cheesewood.''' {{smaller|See ''Pittosporum.''}}—'''Victorian dogwood.''' {{smaller|See ''Prostanthera.''}}—'''Victorian hedge-hyssop, hemp-bush.''' {{smaller|See the nouns.}}—'''Victorian laurel.''' {{smaller|See ''Pittosporum.''}}—'''Victorian lilac.''' {{smaller|See ''Hardenbergia.''}}—'''Victorian myall, parsnip,''' {{smaller|etc. See the nouns.}}—'''Victorian swamp-oak.''' {{smaller|See ''Viminaria.''}}—'''Victorian swampweed.''' {{smaller|See ''swamp-weed.''}}—'''Victorian whitewood.''' {{smaller|See ''Pittosporum.''}}—'''Victorian whortleberry.''' {{smaller|See ''whortleberry.''}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' One living in the reign of Queen Victoria, especially an author.

